[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the physical/link layer interface carrying the EBGP peering between leaf_router_0_0 and spine_router_0_1. On leaf_router_0_0, interface eth1 (the uplink to spine_router_0_1) is administratively and physically down, so the BGP session cannot form. Evidence: on leaf_router_0_0, “ip link show dev eth1” reports state DOWN (no UP flag), and ethtool shows “Link detected: no.” Consequently, there is no connected route for 172.16.0.8/31, and pings to 172.16.0.8 fail with “Network is unreachable.” FRR on the leaf shows the neighbor 172.16.0.8 in Active with 0 prefixes, confirming the session is down. In contrast, on spine_router_0_1, eth1 is UP/LOWER_UP with link detected yes, so the fault localizes to the leaf side of the link.

Mechanism: with eth1 down on the leaf, the connected /31 is not installed, BGP TCP cannot establish, and the EBGP session remains in Active on the leaf. Traffic continued to flow between pc_0_0 and pc_0_1 via the remaining healthy uplink to spine_router_0_0 (eth0), reducing ECMP to a single path and explaining a brief blip during failover.

Root cause: leaf_router_0_0’s eth1 interface toward spine_router_0_1 is administratively down (or otherwise link-down), breaking the EBGP adjacency.

Corrective fix: on leaf_router_0_0, bring eth1 back up and restore carrier (ip link set dev eth1 up; verify cabling/virtual link). Once eth1 is UP/LOWER_UP, the 172.16.0.8/31 connected route will return and the BGP session to spine_router_0_1 should re-establish automatically. === END ===